%I A402096 #12 Nov 18 2026 16:23:05
%S A402096 0,2,24,3106,24086,366492,1435868,1435868,31309816,559033258
%N A402096 Number of primes of Z[sqrt(2)] reachable from 0 by steps of length at most sqrt(A001481(n)), stepping only on primes.
%C A402096 The element a + b*sqrt(2) is identified with the lattice point (a, b), with the Euclidean distance, as in Li et al.
%C A402096 a + b*sqrt(2) is prime if |a^2 - 2*b^2| is a rational prime or the square of a rational prime congruent to 3 or 5 mod 8.
%C A402096 The vertices are 0 and all primes of Z[sqrt(2)]. Two vertices are joined if they are at most sqrt(k) apart, where k = A001481(n). a(n) is the number of primes in the connected component of 0. 0 is not counted.
%C A402096 The unit group is infinite, so associates are distinct points and each is counted.
%C A402096 Only sums of two squares are used as k, because the graph does not change between consecutive terms of A001481.
%C A402096 For n >= 3 the component is also the component of sqrt(2) = (0, 1).
%C A402096 The component is symmetric under a -> -a and b -> -b, so a(n) = 4*Q(n) - 2*X(n), where Q(n) is the number of its points with a >= 0 and b >= 0 and X(n) the number of those on the axes. Q(n) = 0, 1, 7, 780, 6029, 91643, 359001, 359001, 7827561, 139758668 and X(n) = 0, 1, 2, 7, 15, 40, 68, 68, 214, 707.
%C A402096 a(7) = a(8): the steps of squared length 10 are (+-1, +-3) and (+-3, +-1), and they join no new prime to the component.
%C A402096 The farthest prime of the component is, up to sign, sqrt(2), 1 + 4*sqrt(2), 63 + 76*sqrt(2), 373 + 261*sqrt(2), 2263 + 1659*sqrt(2), 7615 + 5436*sqrt(2) (twice), 64813 + 46381*sqrt(2), 705523 + 499615*sqrt(2), for n = 2..10.
%C A402096 This is the Z[sqrt(2)] version of the Gaussian moat problem. Li et al. conjecture that there is no walk to infinity on the primes of Z[sqrt(2)] with steps of bounded length, i.e., that every a(n) is finite.
%C A402096 a(9) and a(10) were computed for the first quadrant by two independent programs and extended to the plane by the symmetry above; a(1)..a(8) were counted directly.
%H A402096 B. Li et al., <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL25/Miller/miller11.html">Modeling Random Walks to Infinity on Primes in Z[sqrt(2)]</a>, J. Integer Seq. 25 (2022), Article 22.6.1.
%H A402096 C. V. Rodrigues da Cunha, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24168695">Exact prime moats in Z[sqrt(2)] for step lengths up to 4</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%e A402096 For n = 3, k = A001481(3) = 2, the steps have length at most sqrt(2). The primes reached from 0 are (0, +-1), (+-1, +-2), (+-1, +-3), (+-1, +-4), (+-2, +-1), (+-3, 0), (+-3, +-1), which are 24 points, so a(3) = 24. The farthest are (+-1, +-4), at distance sqrt(17).
%o A402096 (Python)
%o A402096 from sympy import isprime
%o A402096 from math import isqrt
%o A402096 def is_p(a, b):
%o A402096     n = abs(a*a - 2*b*b)
%o A402096     if isprime(n): return True
%o A402096     q = isqrt(n)
%o A402096     return q*q == n and q % 8 in (3, 5) and isprime(q)
%o A402096 def a_of_k(k):  # k = A001481(n)
%o A402096     r = isqrt(k)
%o A402096     st = [(x, y) for x in range(-r, r+1) for y in range(-r, r+1) if 0 < x*x + y*y <= k]
%o A402096     seen, stack = {(0, 0)}, [(0, 0)]
%o A402096     while stack:
%o A402096         a, b = stack.pop()
%o A402096         for x, y in st:
%o A402096             p = (a+x, b+y)
%o A402096             if p not in seen and is_p(*p):
%o A402096                 seen.add(p); stack.append(p)
%o A402096     return len(seen) - 1
%o A402096 print([a_of_k(k) for k in (0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 8)])
%Y A402096 Cf. A001481, A055673 (norms of primes of Z[sqrt(2)]), A401602 (analog for Eisenstein primes), A055025 (norms of Gaussian primes).
%K A402096 nonn,more,hard,new
%O A402096 1,2
%A A402096 _Cristian Vitorino Rodrigues da Cunha_, Nov 12 2026
